      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "Subtracting $1$ from both sides, we get $16^{x^2+y}-1=16^{x+y^2}-1=0.$\nWe may apply the difference of squares identity,\n\\[16^{x^2+y}-1=(2^{4x+3})^2-1=(2^{4x+3}-1)(2^{4x+3}+1)=(2^{2x+1}-1)(2^{2x+1}+1)(2^{4x+3}+1)=(2^{2x+1}-1)(2^{4x+2}+2^{4x+1}+2^{4x}+2^{4x-1}+1)(2^{4x+3}+1).\\]\nThe equation $16^{x^2+y}+16^{x+y^2}=1$ then reduces to\n\\[(2^{2x+1}-1)(2^{4x+2}+2^{4x+1}+2^{4x}+2^{4x-1}+1)(2^{4x+3}+1) = 0.\\]\nHence, the equation has the three solutions \\[x=-\\frac{1}{2n+1} \\quad \\text{for $n\\in \\mathbb Z$},\\]\n\\[x\\square y = -1,\\]\n\\[2x+1\\square 1 = -\\frac{1}{5}.\\]",
