    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We have the equation\n\\[16^{x^2 + y} + 16^{x + y^2} = 1.\\]\nLet $a = x^2 + y$ and $b = x + y^2$.  Then $a + b = x^2 + y + x + y^2 = (x + y)(x + y) + x + y^2 + y = (x + y)(x + y + 1)$.\nWe see that $a = b$, so $a = b = x^2 + x + y^2 + 1$.  Therefore,\n\\[(a + 1)^2 + 16^{a + 1} = 1 + a + b + 1 = 1 + (a + b) + 1 = 3.\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[(x + 1)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 1,\\]\nwhich is equivalent to\n\\[(x + 1)^2 + (y - 1)^2 + 2y + 2 = 1,\\]\nwhich is equivalent to\n\\[(x + 1)^2 + (y - 1)^2 \\ge 0.\\]\nHence, $1$ and $-1$ are possible values of $x + y^2$.  If $x + y^2 = -1$, then $y^2 = -x - 1$ and $-1 \\le x \\le -2$.  If $x + y^2 = 1$, then $y^2 = x + 1$ and $-1 \\le x \\le 1$.\nIf $x \\ge -1$, then $y^2 = x + 1$, so $-1 \\le x \\le -1 + \\sqrt{1 + x} = -1 + \\sqrt{1 + x}$.  Since $x \\ge -1$, we allow $x = -1$ and still have $1 \\le x + y^2 \\le 2$.\nBy similar reasoning, if $x \\le -2$, then $-2 + \\sqrt{1 + x} \\le x + y^2 \\le -1$.\nIf $x \\in [-1, -1 + \\sqrt{1 + x}]$, then $-1 + \\sqrt{1 + x} \\le y^2 \\le x + 1$, so\n$-1 + \\sqrt{1 + x} \\le y \\le \\sqrt{x + 1}$.\nSimilarly, if $x \\in [-1 + \\sqrt{1 + x}, 1]$, then $1 \\le y \\le 1 + \\sqrt{1 + x}$.  Hence,\n\\[\\boxed{0 \\text{ solutions}}.\\]The answer is: 0",
